A||||8||Gary W||How not to fix your brakes||||12||
Z||000000||Gary W||11-11-2001||09:56 PM||||Freeing up the frozen calipers on my parked-for-2-year GT, i used a c-clamp to compress the caliper to get it off. I wanted to check the piston and seal, but I couldn't get a grip to pull it out. So, I just gave a tiny shot of compressed air into the bleeder hole.... POP!<P>I finally found the piston, and it's not damaged. Thankfully, I'm usually not this dumb.||||reg||12
Z||000001||DJRice||11-11-2001||10:03 PM||||&lt;mom&gt;<BR>"Be careful, you could put someone's eye out with that thing."<BR>&lt;/mom&gt;<P>--Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000002||SKIDMARK||11-11-2001||11:44 PM||||The blow gun is OK if you put the old outboard pad in the caliper and put a rag between the piston and the pad. Oh yea, and keep your fingers out of the way too! Throw some rags over it just in case.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||11-12-2001||02:26 AM||||and brake fluid getting in your eyes or your favorite pin up calender.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-12-2001||03:25 AM||||"WOAH! These are spring loaded!"   <P>Been there, done that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Gary W:<BR><B>Thankfully, I'm usually not this dumb.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||FieroJoe||11-12-2001||01:25 PM||||We did that a while back trying to fix FieroMaster88's clutch slave cylinder.  With a nice pop, she flew about 10 feet and luckily landed in a bag of papers or something.<P>Also, about 2 months ago I was restoring an 84 SE for a guy (he was paying me to do it, just fix it up so everything works), and the left front caliper was binding up.  So, I tried to use compressed air to push the piston out, but I didn't have the correct type of blowgun that would make a good enough seal into the caliper.  I finally just attached it back to the brake system and pumped the brakes until it came out.  Couldn't get the stupid piston back in either after cleaning it, becuase the o-ring was so screwed.  $8 for a rebuild kit, or $12.99 for a new caliper.  I just went with 2 new calipers and that was that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Leadfoot||11-12-2001||04:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Gary W:<BR>[B]I finally found the piston, and it's not damaged. [B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Good luck putting it back in.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
